{"ex-E403-1":{"number":"1","title":"Half-value thickness","question":"<p>A patient wears a shield apron to protect her lower abdomen from ionizing radiation when taking a chest X-ray (Fig a). Figure b shows how the intensity of an X-ray beam changes as the beam travels through the material making the apron.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/76fc960d0edc71c200f45c64.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:343.7px\"/></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/380b3f01691e6878a35eb14b.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:350.0px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the half-value thickness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>½</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of the material.</p><p>(b) If an X-ray beam of initial intensity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> passes through a distance of 2<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>½</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> in the material, what will its intensity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> be?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) 7.8 × 10<sup>−3</sup> cm</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Read the value of x corresponding to 0.5I<sub>0</sub>.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E403  |  Source PDF p. 5","source":{"name":"E403 Examples.docx","sha256":"30ec5c3b09c767f3b6b533171069ce3a92bc6531d496215177b963cf1a1c6f6b","block":3},"id":"ex-E403-1","chapter":"E403"},"ex-E403-2":{"number":"2","title":"Linear attenuation coefficient","question":"<p>The figure below shows an X-ray image of a human hand. It is given that the linear attenuation coefficients of muscle and bone are 0.24 cm<sup>−1</sup> and 0.51 cm<sup>−1</sup> respectively.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/1b75ec76d1ed719f4f96415c.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:426.6px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the percentage of incoming intensity of X-rays that passes through</p><p>(i) muscle of thickness of 1.5 cm (fleshly part of palm),</p><p>(ii) a bone of thickness of 1 cm (the finger).</p><p>(b) How will the result in (a)(ii) change if X-rays of higher energy are employed? Explain your answer.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) (i) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>e<sup>−μx</sup>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = e<sup>−μx</sup> = e<sup>−0.24</sup> <sup>×</sup> <sup>1.5</sup> = 0.698</p><p>Percentage of intensity = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 100%</p><p>= 0.698 × 100%</p><p>= 69.8%</p><p>(ii) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>e<sup>−μx</sup>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = e<sup>−μx</sup> = e<sup>−0.51</sup> <sup>×</sup> <sup>1</sup> = 0.600</p><p>Percentage of intensity = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 100%</p><p>= 0.600 × 100%</p><p>= 60.0%</p><p>(b) X-rays of higher energy have a higher penetrating power. Therefore, a larger percentage of X-rays will pass through the bone, i.e. the result in</p><p>(a)(ii) will increase.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E403  |  Source PDF p. 8","source":{"name":"E403 Examples.docx","sha256":"30ec5c3b09c767f3b6b533171069ce3a92bc6531d496215177b963cf1a1c6f6b","block":17},"id":"ex-E403-2","chapter":"E403"},"ex-E403-3":{"number":"3","title":"X-ray image","question":"<p>A patient takes an artificial contrast medium before taking an X-ray image of his stomach. The X-ray image is shown below.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/3e733897819c4be9e3980282.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:374.2px\"/></p><p>(a) Explain the function of the artificial contrast medium in taking the image shown in the figure.</p><p>(b) An abnormality indicated by the red arrow is found in the X-ray image, endoscopy is then used to examine the stomach.</p><p>(i) Explain why X-ray imaging is suitable to be used at the beginning for screening for abnormalities.</p><p>(ii) State one disadvantage of using endoscopy over X-ray imaging.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) The artificial contrast medium fills the stomach and increases the attenuation of X-rays in passing through the stomach significantly. Therefore, the stomach appears lighter than normal in the image and becomes easier to be distinguished from the surrounding tissues.</p><p>(b) (i) X-ray imaging is fast and cheap. It does not require any preparation before imaging.</p><p>(ii) Endoscopy is an invasive procedure. The patient may feel uncomfortable during the procedure.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E403  |  Source PDF p. 19","source":{"name":"E403 Examples.docx","sha256":"30ec5c3b09c767f3b6b533171069ce3a92bc6531d496215177b963cf1a1c6f6b","block":38},"id":"ex-E403-3","chapter":"E403"},"ex-E403-4":{"number":"4","title":"Matrix size","question":"<p>The CT image in Figure a is the first patient image of the brain scanned on the prototype scanner in 1971 and has a matrix size of 80 × 80. Figure b is a modern CT image of the brain.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/e8f912725e7e1ea8b8056434.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:343.3px\"/></p><p>If the image size of the modern CT image is 32 cm × 32 cm and the size of each pixel of the image is 0.0977 mm<sup>2</sup>, what is its matrix size?</p>","answer":"<p>Length of a pixel = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> = 0.3126 mm</p><p>Number of pixels along one dimension = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>length of image</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>length of pixel</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1024</p><p>∴ Its matrix size is 1024 × 1024.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E403  |  Source PDF p. 26","source":{"name":"E403 Examples.docx","sha256":"30ec5c3b09c767f3b6b533171069ce3a92bc6531d496215177b963cf1a1c6f6b","block":50},"id":"ex-E403-4","chapter":"E403"},"ex-E403-6":{"number":"6","title":"Effective half-life","question":"<p>The physical and biological half-lives of iodine-131 are 8 days and 138 days respectively.</p><p>(a) What is the effective half-life <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of iodine-131?</p><p>(b) A patient receives iodine-131 for radionuclide imaging. Find the time needed for the amount of radionuclides remaining in his body to drop to 10%.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>b</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> ,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>b</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>)<sup>–1</sup> = (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>)<sup>–1</sup> = 7.56 days</p><p>(b) Decay constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>e<sup>−kt</sup>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> ln <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> ln 0.1 = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> ln 0.1 = 25.1 days</p><p>∴ The time needed is 25.1 days.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book E4  |  Chapter E403  |  Source PDF p. 39","source":{"name":"E403 Examples.docx","sha256":"30ec5c3b09c767f3b6b533171069ce3a92bc6531d496215177b963cf1a1c6f6b","block":59},"id":"ex-E403-6","chapter":"E403"}}